   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1.1 Context\n",
    "\n",
    "In early January of 2026, a winter storm hit The Netherlands with below freezing temperatures and snow up to around 20 cm. The most affected area, as seen in the figure below, was the area around Utrecht. [SOURCE]\n",
    "\n",
    "![alt text](storm.png)\n",
    "\n",
    "As seen in the timeline below, the storm/cold weather started on January 2 and lasted until about January 11, although the worst of it occurred January 6, with temperatures going as low as -10C in the early morning.\n",
    "\n",
    "![alt text](<Winter Storm Timeline.png>)\n",
    "\n",
    "The main aspect of the winter weather that causes trouble for rail transit is the freezing temperatures. When temperatures get as low as they did on January 6, it can cause ice to form on the tracks and catenary wires above the tracks. Ice on the tracks creates issues for the rail switches as they can literally freeze in place. Areas with colder climates that experience these issues more often have ways to mitigate these effects, mainly through heating the rails. Additionally, ice on the catenary wires can interfere with electricity transfer between the train and the wires.\n",
    "These issues notably affected Utrecht Centraal (How do we know this?). Utrecht Centraal is the largest rail station in The Netherlands by passenger throughput [SOURCE] and is located right where the coldest temperatures were during the storm. Additionally, given the high passenger numbers, there is a large amount of rail infrastructure at the station, including around 70 rail switches, which are vulnerable to the cold temperatures.\n",
    "\n",
    "[4] Oorsprong, J., Faber, J.:Station utrecht centraal in ontwikkeling (Jun 2021), https://koerstue.nl/koersief/online/previously-on-the-koersief/utrechtcs \\## 1.1 Context\n"
   ]
  },
