    "## 2.2 Data Used"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The datasets used as input in this project were acquired from KNMI [1] and Rijden de Treinen [2]. The weather dataset specifically was from the weather station De Bilt, which was chosen as the representative proxy of weather patterns around Utrecht Centraal due to its relatively close distance to the station.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 2.2.1 Weather dataset\n",
    "The weather dataset contained detailed hourly records of weather records per day at De Bilt (weather station 260), 4 km northeast of Utrecht Centraal, including but not limited to:\n",
    "- Temperature\n",
    "- Dew point\n",
    "- Precipitation amount and duration\n",
    "- Snow occurence (weather code)\n",
    "- Wind speed\n",
    "\n",
    "Initially, the raw data was loaded from the CSV file, and unnecessary formatting characters and whitespace were removed from the column names using Claude Sonnet 5.5 Medium, making sure to keep missing data traceable.\n",
    "\n",
    "Next, the date and time information was processed to create a datetime index for each hourly observation. Since KNMI records its hourly observations using hour-ending UTC timestamps (1–24), one hour was subtracted to represent the actual start of each observation period. The weather measurements were then converted into standard units to make them easier to interpret. Temperature and dew point were converted from tenths of degrees Celsius to degrees Celsius, precipitation from tenths of millimetres to millimetres, and wind speed from tenths of metres per second to kilometres per hour. Precipitation values recorded as -1, representing trace amounts, were treated as zero. A binary snow indicator was also created to identify hours with recorded snowfall.\n",
    "\n",
    "The dataset was subsequently sorted chronologically and restricted to the study period 2–11 January for each year from 2021 to 2026. This allowed the weather conditions in January 2026 to be compared with the same calendar period across the five preceding years (2021–2025), which was deemed a sufficiently large sample for the purposes of this research. Each observation was also assigned a day index, indicating its position within the selected 10-day period.\n",
    "\n",
    "Finally, several weather indicators were calculated at both daily and yearly levels. Daily indicators included average temperature, average dew point, total precipitation, average wind speed and the number of hours with snowfall. Yearly indicators summarised the entire 10-day period for each year and included average and minimum temperatures, total precipitation, average wind speed, and snowfall hours.\n",
    "\n",
    "These calculations produced two datasets: one containing daily weather indicators for comparing corresponding days across years, and another containing overall weather indicators for comparing the full 10-day periods. The 2021–2025 observations were then separated to serve as the historical baseline against which the weather conditions (snowstorm event) in January 2026 could be evaluated.\n",
    "\n",
    "Note that two CSV weather files were created in the analysis process, one with only the data from 2026 (weather_seleceted.csv) and another with the full dataset from 2021 to 2026 (weather_total_cleaned.csv). This was done out of practicality and purely served the purpose of maintaing overview and debugging. Besides the difference in filtered years, the CSV files are identical. \n",
    "\n",
    "## 2.2.2 Train dataset\n",
    "The train dataset contained detailed records of train arrivals, departures, delays and cancellations, including:\n",
    "- Service type (e.g. Sprinter, InterCity)\n",
    "- Cancellation type (completely, partly)\n",
    "- Maximum delay for a certain service\n",
    "- Arrival delay\n",
    "- Departure delay\n",
    "- Arrival or departure cancelled\n",
    "\n",
    "The dataset was first restricted to the study period of 15 December 2025 to 30 April 2026. Unnecessary columns were removed, and the remaining columns were renamed to improve clarity and consistency. The analysis was limited to Intercity and Sprinter services, excluding all other train types.\n",
    "\n",
    "Next, scheduled arrival and departure times were converted into a consistent hour-and-minute format. Actual arrival and departure times were then calculated by adding the recorded delays (in minutes) to their respective scheduled times. Based on these actual times, the dataset was restricted to train stops where both arrival and departure occurred between 05:00 and 22:59. The data was subsequently sorted by date and actual arrival time.\n",
    "\n",
    "Several additional variables were created to support the analysis. Binary indicators were introduced to identify whether a train stop experienced an arrival or departure delay, with separate indicators identifying delays of at least three minutes. A cancellation indicator was also created, classifying a stop as cancelled if either its arrival or departure was cancelled. Missing cancellation values were treated as not cancelled. Additionally, a weekly variable was generated to group observations by calendar week.\n",
    "\n",
    "Finally, to prevent days with unusually high cancellation activity from disproportionately influencing the baseline analysis, a filtering procedure was applied. Daily cancellation totals were calculated, and the 90th percentile was used as the exclusion threshold. Days with cancellation counts strictly above this threshold were removed, while days at or below the threshold were retained. This filtering step was optional and could be disabled to retain all days."
   ]
