   "source": [
    "## 3.2 Train analysis"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The train data usage, cleaning and modification is described in section 2.2. For background, see /background/period_selection.ipynb and background/train_analysis.py.\n",
    "\n",
    "The train analysis creates a baseline for the comparison analysis; an initial data pipeline is created to identify the generic indicators and insights which can be retreived from the data.\n",
    "\n",
    "The three indicators and justification used are as follows:\n",
    "- Delay indicator: \n",
    "    - Every arrival- and departure delay is flagged if the delay column > 0. \n",
    "    - An additional binary indicator is more important, however, which is the arrival and departure delay being bigger or equal to three (3) minutes or 180 seconds. This is the delay value indicated in NS' performance agreements as communicated to passengers and stakeholders [3].\n",
    "- Number of cancellations:\n",
    "    - A train is cancelled if either arrival or departure is cancelled.\n",
    "    - Cancellations can be per hour, day, week of day or the average over the analysis period.\n",
    "- Punctuality performance:\n",
    "    - Punctuality of a train is the number of on-time events (on-time indicating delays being smaller than 180 seconds) divided by the total scheduled events.\n",
    "    - Punctuality can differ at arrival, departure and a total punctuality figure.\n",
    "    - Keeping non-cancelled trains in this dataset\n",
    "\n",
    "The delay indicator entails how much and how severe a disruption is, where the number of cancellations complement this. Punctuality answers the question how often the services meet the performance standard. The delay measures the magnitude and distribution of operational disruption, whereas the punctuality measures the proportion of services which meet a predefined performance standard."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 3.2.1 Delays and cancellations in numbers"
