  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Across the full period, 25% of the trains experienced a delay greater than zero minutes, while around 10% were delayed by at least three minutes. This indicates that minor delays were relatively common. On average, each vehicle is delayed by 1.22 minutes per vehicle, while the total delay-minutes per day accumualted to 845.5 minutes in the baseline. The cancellations per 100 vehicles in the baseline equals 9.3 vehicles per 100. These results show that operational disruptions involvee both delays and cancellations.\n",
    "\n",
    "The figure (Fig. 3.2.1) shows the daily percentage of delayed trains in the time-period, for delays above 0 minutes and delays of 3 minutes or more. Thin lines show daily values and bold lines show the smoothed rolling trend. Arrivals are delayed more often than departures (mean 18.2% vs 16.8% for 0 minutes and 7.9% vs 6.6% for above 3 minutes), an early indicator that part of the arrival delay is absorbed during the stop. \n",
    "\n",
    "Delays vary over time; the lowest levels occur around the year turnover, after implementation of the new timetable, and are followed by a sustained peak from February to end of February. Here, arrival delays reach around 28% and 13% (>0 min), from about 12% and 5% in December (>= 3 min). The thin lines indicate that delays fluctuate strongly, so the trend is the better indicator along the mean values."
   ]
