  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Figure 3.2.2 shows the mean delay in minutes per hour for arrival and departure, the frequency (Fig 3.2.3) of delays in percentage for arrival and departure (> 0 min and >= 3 min), as well as the cancellation rate per hour (Fig 3.2.4). \n",
    "\n",
    "Delays for a clear daily pattern, where mean arrival delay is lowest in the early morning and around midday (0.45 min and 0.52 min), where it especially rises thorugh the afternoon, peaking in the hour of 16:00 - 17:00 with a mean arrival delay of around 0.9 minutes. An additional peak occurs at 22:00 - 23:00. The share of delayed trains show the same shape: arrivals delayed by more than 0 minutes range from about 15% to 22% in a long afternoon/early evening peak. The afternoon peak coincides with the evening rush, suggesting that the delays build up during the day through knock-on effects and higher loads. Departure delays are consistenly lower than arrivals, in both mean delay and frequency, which again indicate delay absorption during stops using dwell times. \n",
    "\n",
    "The cancellation rate  follows a different pattern; it seems so build up into the afternoon and decreases before the evening rush. Cancellations peak before delay peak hours. A possible explanation is that cancellations are used to prevent propagation or reflect off-peak adjustments, although this cannot be concluded from the figure.\n",
    "\n",
    "The number of delayed trains are highest in the morning peak and the afternoon peak (Fig 3.2.6)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 3.2.3 Punctuality\n",
    "\n",
    "Punctuality discarded cancellations in the dataset; cancelled trains cannot be punctual."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 40,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
