    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Trains punctual at both arrival and departure: 90.22%\n",
      "Trains punctual at arrival: 91.80%\n",
      "Trains punctual at departure: 93.26%\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "analyse_punctuality(train)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The plot (Fig. 3.2.7 and Fig. 3.2.8) shows train punctuality by hour of day and weekday. Departures are more punctual than arrivals in (nearly) all hours and on all days (mean 92.9% vs 91.5%), indicating possible delay absorption stops.\n",
    "\n",
    "Punctuality is highest in the early morning between 05:00 and 06:00 and lowest in the afternoon rush-peak between 16:00 and 19:00, with arrival punctuality dropping to around 89%. In the evening, little delay is recovered. Across the week, Tuesday is the least punctual day in the baseline (~89% on arrival and ~91% on departure), whereas Sunday is the most punctual. Weekend days generally perform better than weekdays. The smallest arrival-departure gap is on Friday, and highest on Tuesdays.\n",
    "\n",
    "At train level, of the almost 9000 trains with a delay at arrival of departure, 54% are delayed at both, 30% are delayed only at arrival and 17% are delayed only at departure, generating delay. Of all delayed arrivals, about 36% are recovered before departure.\n",
    "\n",
    "Train punctuality of departures is consistently higher than punctuality at arrivals; the mean values confirm this. This is cause for the investigation of delay absorption of Utrecht Centraal."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 3.2.4 Delay absorption and propagation\n",
    "\n",
    "The first plot (Fig. 3.2.9) shows delay recovery or delay increase in per weekday and per hour. The left plots show all delayed trains using a net-delay-change and the right plots show delay absorption of late-arriving trains only.\n",
    "- In the left panels, blue bars indicate a net delay reduction at the stop and the red bars a net delay increase.\n",
    "- In the right panels, the blue indicates trains whose delay is absorbed and red trains whose delay propagated.\n",
    "\n",
    "The net delay change equals the difference between the departure and arrival delay; if net delay change is bigger than zero delays are absorbed or dissolved:\n",
    "- departure delay - departure delay is the actual arrival delay removed.\n",
    "\n",
    "The delay absorption equals the difference between the departure and arrival delay, divided by the departure delay. \n",
    "- (arrival delay - departure delays) / arrival delays gives the share of arrival delay removed\n",
    "\n",
    "On average, the net delay change is slightly negative (-0.08 min/train), so stops increase delay by a very small, immeasurable, margin. The value per train is small because most trains have no delay to recover. Measured in delay minutes, 12.2% of arrival delay is absorbed, rising to 31.0% for late-arriving trains. From this point on absorption is only calculated for late-arriving trains, as trains arriving on time have no delay to absorb and dilute the statistic. The net delay change is calcualted for all trains, as it also captures delay which is generated by the station.\n",
    "\n",
    "The plots first column on the statistics of the net delay reduction is an indicative plot showing the minimalistic influence of net delay change on the total dataset; the delay averages down to zero in a sufficiently large dataset.\n",
    "\n",
    "Absorption is an interesting statistic to show in this stage. Absorption of late trains is highest on Wednesdays (around 36%) and lowest on Fridays (around 27%). By hour, absorption is highest is highest in the early morning and decreases to around 25% in the afternoon. The recovery of capacity is higher in the morning than in the afternoon peak."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 41,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Mean net delay change (all running trains): -0.09 min/train\n",
      "Late-arrival delay absorption: 31.76%\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
