   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The bottom plot (Fig. 3.2.10) shows delay change for each weekday and departure hour. Only trains whose delay changes between arrival and departure are included (non-cancelled trains with a known arrival and departure delay, and a non-zero delay change). The blue bars give the share of these trains that leave with less delay than they arrived with; the red bars are the share that leave with more delay. Both shares add up to 100%. The black dots show the balance, which equals the net direction in absorption of propagation.\n",
    "\n",
    "On every weekday, the majority of trains with a changing delay reduces delay (59-68%). The same trend can be observed on hourly basis.\n",
    "\n",
    "The bottom plot shows trains, whereas the top figure weigh each train by its delay minutes. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The net delay change and delay absorption show three things:\n",
    "1. The stop acts on balance as a recovery point: reduction is the most frequent outcome at all days and hours, which is consistent with the positive delay absorption (31.0% of arrivals).\n",
    "2. Recovery is not complete nor reliable: roughly 1/3th of the trains with a changing delay leave with more delay than they arrived with.\n",
    "3. The recovery capacity is lowest in the afternoon peak and at the end of the service day, which are also the periods with the lowest punctuality. Indicating these are critical sections in the base selection. Tuesday has the lowest punctuality with the lowest delay absorption in the weekdays.\n",
    "\n",
    "Use Net delay change (min/train) to answer if the stop, on average, contributes to delay accumulation or absorption (operational effect).\n",
    "- Use instead of delay absorption as the denominator for arrival delay in absorption can produce misleading or extreme percentages.\n",
    "- Does not indicate if dwell time improved the delay\n",
    "- Use when: comparing overall effect of dwell time, compare departure hours or weekdays for all trains, assess overall performance.\n",
    "\n",
    "Use Delay absorption (%) to answer how effectively existing arrival delay is recovered during the stop.\n",
    "- Use when: investigate whether late trains recover existing delays, which dwell times help late trains recover"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 3.2.5 Correlation and dwell-time heatmaps"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Section 3.2.4 showed that a stop at Utrecht Centraal keeps a net delay equal and reduces delays for late arrivals. Additionally, it showed that this effect shos by weekday and per hour. This section looks at two things; how strongly the weekly operational metrics are related and to what extent the scheduled dwell time at Utrecht Centraal determines or explains the absorption.\n",
    "\n",
    "Dwell time determines the available buffer for trains to keep on time. \n",
    "\n",
    "Three heatmaps are used: a weekly correlation matrix, the delay absorption of late-arriving trains by dwell time and departure hour and the mean net delay change of all trains by dwell time and departure hour.\n",
    "- Heatmaps with less than 15 trains in the corridor are greyed out."
   ]
  },
  {
