  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### 1) Weekly correlation (Fig. 3.2.11)\n",
    "\n",
    "Weekly arrival and departure delays are almost perfectly correlated (r = 0.92), which is almost trivial. Weeks with many late arrivals also have many late departures.\n",
    "\n",
    "The cancellation rate has a moderate positive correlation with delays, so poor weeks tend to be poor in arrival and departure delays. The number of scheduled trains is practically unrelated to delays and weakly related to cancellations (r = -0.16). Disruptive weeks probably drive both delays and cancellations.\n",
    "\n",
    "##### 2) Delay absorption heatmap (Fig. 3.2.12)\n",
    "\n",
    "Absorption among late-arriving trains depends mainly on the scheduled dwell time. For a dwell time of 0-1 minute, absorption is negative in all hours with sufficient data (-14% to -33% increase in delay), so these trains leave with more delay than they arrive with. At a scheduled dwell time of 2 minutes the absorption nears zero and steadily rises afterwards from 18% at 3 minutes to 72% at 8-9 minutes.\n",
    "\n",
    "The departure hour has a second effect. For the same dwell time, the absorption is the highest in the early morning and lowest in the early afternoon; except for scheduled dwell time between 0-1 minutes. The higher afternoon absorption and lower peak-hour absorption (propagation) reflect higher loads on the station and trains in the peak-hours.\n",
    "\n",
    "##### 3) Net delay change heatmap (Fig. 3.2.13)\n",
    "\n",
    "The net delay change show the same gradient as in 2) but in minutes per train. For 0-1 minutes of dwell time, net delay change is positive in all hours (+0.8 to +1.8 min), so delay is generated at these stops. At 2-4 minutes the net change is close to zero and from 4 minutes onwards the net delay change is negative.\n",
    "\n",
    "The delays turn positive in the afternoon, as in line with the low absorption in the previous heatmap. For long dwell times in the late evening, the net delay is positive; these cells may reflect turnaround or other out of time events and must be treated with caution. \n",
    "\n",
    "The small average net change (-0.08 min/train) in Section 3.2.4 clearly hides two opposing groups; trains with a short scheduled dwell time which add delay and trains with a longer dwell time which absorb delay."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The following conclusions can be drawn:\n",
    "1) Delay absorption is mainly determined by the buffer in the timetable. Below 3 minutes of scheduled dwell time, the stop produces delay. Above 5 minutes it removes the largest share of delays.\n",
    "2) At equal dwell times, the absorption is lower in the afternoon peak. A loaded network thus reduces the effectiveness of the buffer.\n",
    "3) Weekly delays and cancellations move together.\n",
    "4) Differences between weekdays and hours in absorption and net change are both dependent on the buffer and network load, not solely on the level of incoming delay."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
