  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 3.2.7 Train conclusions\n",
    "\n",
    "Over the baseline study period, delays are short and mostly small. The mean arrival delay is within the minute per train, depending on the hour. More than 90% of the trains arrive and depart within 3 minutes of schedule. Of the approximately 9,000 trains with a delay at either arrival or departure, 30% are delayed only at arrival. Cancellations vary strongly throughout the day, but most occur in the afternoon.\n",
    "\n",
    "**A typical day** therefore starts with high punctuality and few delays. Delays gradually build up through the day, peak in the afternoon rush period (16:00 - 19:00) and increase again in the late evening. Cancellations peak earlier than delays, already in the early afternoon.\n",
    "\n",
    "Across the week, Tuesday is the least punctual day and Sunday the most punctual. Across the period, delays are lowest around the turn of the year and highest in mid-February, after which they stabilize. Under normal conditions a delay is small, recurring and has a predictable temporal pattern. Large deviations from this pattern coincide with disruptions and are accompanied by increased cancellations.\n",
    "\n",
    "The stop of Utrecht Centraal is a recovery point, but only to a limited extent. For late arriving trains, aprroximately one-third of the arrival delay can be removed. This recovery mainly occurs when the timetable provides sufficient scheduled dwell time, particularly at least three minutes, while recovery is more limited during the busy afternoon peak. Trains with a scheduled dwell time of 0-2 minutes systematically accumulate additional delay.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "However, differences in delay abosorption and net delay change between hours and weekdays should not be attributed solely to the level of incoming delay. THey are influenced by the interaction between the available buffer time and the operational network load. Periods with greater network pressure, such as the afternoon peak, may therefore provide fewer opportunities to use available dwell time for recovery. The most direct operational lever for improving punctuality at Utrecht Centraal is consequently the provision of sufficient timetable buffer, particularly during periods of high network load."
   ]
  },
