  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Figure 3.4.1 shows the cancellation and punctuality per hour compared between the baseline scenario and the snowstorm disrupted scenario. The cancellations are increased with the biggest margin, where on the worst hours cancellations rise from < 10% up until above 60% of the trains cancelled. The arrival and departure punctuality decreased from around 92% to 85% in punctuality, indicating that the trains which were not cancelled, both have lower arrival and departure punctuality in operations.\n",
    "\n",
    "Figure 3.4.2 displays the same satistics per weekday, for which the same cancellation statistics can be observed. In the arrival and departure punctualities, the most impacted days can be identified easily in the graph. The departure punctuality reduces from 94% to 47% on monday.\n",
    "\n",
    "Finally, (Fig 3.4.2) the delay size and absorption have been visualized. In the baseline, <20% of the trains are delayed, whereas in the snowstorm period this is almost equal to 30%. This trend is consistenly higher than the baseline scenario, rather intuitively indicating that the delay and puntuality statistics are in agreement. The delay absorption of late-arriving trains is plotted against the scheduled dwell time in minutes. It can be observed that there is an increased capability of absorbing delays in longer scheduled dwell times in the baseline versus the snowstorm scenarios. However, the constraints may restrict fair results.\n",
    "\n",
    "The table shows difference of the indicators is in percentage points, whereas change is relative change compared to baseline. The KPI comparison Table confirms a substantial deterioration in operational performance during the snowstorm period. The average number of delay minutes per vehicle increased by 87.3%, while the cancellation rate rose from 7.5% to 44.5% (+37.0 percentage points). Arrival and departure punctuality both decreased by approximately 7 percentage points. Furthermore, delay absorption decreased from 31.8% to 13.4%, indicating a reduced ability to recover delays within the remaining train operations. Overall, the results show that the snowstorm affected both the continuity of train services and the performance of trains that remained in operation."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The high cancellation rate highlights that the performance indicators (net delay change, delay absorption and punctuality) do not tell the full story and are strongly influenced by the number of trains that remain in operation. When anticipating adverse weather conditions, ProRail and NS may proactively reduce available capacity or adjust the timetable to maintain more manageable operations. As a result, fewer trains operate on the network, potentially improving the performance indicators of the remaining services. Therefore, these indicators should be considered alongside the cancellation rate to obtain a more complete picture of the operational impact of weather disruptions."
   ]
  },
